1
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:14,240
Let's jump in to talk about
the Internet protocol.

2
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:19,340
I'm assuming everybody
knows what the Internet is.

3
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:21,260
You've been able to
reach these videos,

4
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,840
but do you really know
all the details about it?

5
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,550
Here we'll provide at
least the foundations

6
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:30,795
to make sure we're
all on the same page,

7
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:34,250
and set up the rest
of the module.

8
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:40,650
Internetworking. The real origin

9
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:44,145
of why do we need
"The Internet",

10
00:00:44,145 --> 00:00:47,130
is being able to enable

11
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:52,070
communication between multiple
heterogeneous networks.

12
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:57,090
We talked about the case where
we might have somebody on

13
00:00:57,090 --> 00:00:59,830
a 5G network needing
to be able to talk to

14
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:03,550
somebody on an Ethernet network.

15
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:06,950
There was a wide collection of

16
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:13,185
different local area network
types or link types,

17
00:01:13,185 --> 00:01:15,750
but these different
disparate networks

18
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:18,510
need to be able to
communicate with one another.

19
00:01:18,510 --> 00:01:23,570
In a key paper from 1974
from the Surf and Con,

20
00:01:23,570 --> 00:01:25,880
called A protocol
for Packet Network

21
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:27,230
In Our Communication,

22
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:29,430
they introduced this concept of

23
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:32,845
a router at the edge
of each network.

24
00:01:32,845 --> 00:01:40,860
The Internet Protocol
effectively came out of that.

25
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:47,930
It was called a gateway in
that paper to start with.

26
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:50,770
One of the things to know about

27
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:56,050
the Internet protocol
is the service model.

28
00:01:56,050 --> 00:01:59,495
In particular it's best effort.

29
00:01:59,495 --> 00:02:05,470
This means that the delivery
packets may get dropped.

30
00:02:05,470 --> 00:02:07,830
The timing, there's
no guarantee on

31
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:10,690
how long it takes to
deliver a packet.

32
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:15,390
The order packets may get
reordered in the network.

33
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:18,110
The reasoning behind
all of this, is that,

34
00:02:18,110 --> 00:02:21,620
interconnecting
different link layers

35
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:24,070
needs to be really at

36
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:27,150
the lowest common denominator if

37
00:02:27,150 --> 00:02:30,670
one at the link layer

38
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:33,850
can support reliable delivery
but the other can't.

39
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:37,520
Well, the thing that
interconnects them isn't going to

40
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,800
be able to guarantee
reliable delivery.

41
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,760
This is this lowest
common denominator of

42
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:48,200
the things it wants to
be able to interconnect.

43
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,560
Here, I've been using the term
packet in the link layer.

44
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,460
We use the term frame for

45
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:00,120
the message that
it's going to be

46
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:03,660
sending at the Internet
or at the network layer,

47
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:11,250
we call it a packet looking
at addressing an IP.

48
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:14,690
Each interface in a network

49
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:18,910
or on a device will
get an IP address.

50
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:21,275
Shown on the right is

51
00:03:21,275 --> 00:03:25,030
an example where you
have two laptops.

52
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:27,610
They each have their
own IP address

53
00:03:27,610 --> 00:03:29,470
and then there's
some network devices

54
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:32,810
with different ports on

55
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:35,605
it and they each have
their own IP addresses.

56
00:03:35,605 --> 00:03:39,630
All right. The IPv4 version

57
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:43,710
four which is the
longest standing,

58
00:03:43,710 --> 00:03:49,800
most common used
for IP addresses.

59
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:54,175
The addresses are 32 bits long,

60
00:03:54,175 --> 00:03:58,530
and you typically see them
in dotted decimal form.

61
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:00,145
You would see an address like

62
00:04:00,145 --> 00:04:08,690
192.168.0.1 This is the
groupings of eight bits.

63
00:04:08,690 --> 00:04:12,170
Each of the eight
bits in that 32 bits,

64
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:15,470
gets one of these
decimal numbers.

65
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:20,440
Then each one of
them can be 0-255.

66
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:26,830
IPv6, or version 6
is also now growing

67
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:28,910
in popularity because of

68
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:33,300
the limited address
space in IPv4,

69
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:38,050
but also the sprawling
number of addresses we need.

70
00:04:38,330 --> 00:04:44,670
Here the addresses are
expanded to be 128 bits.

71
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:49,590
Here we need to have what
you would commonly see is

72
00:04:49,590 --> 00:04:54,265
groupings of 16 bits
separated by a colon.

73
00:04:54,265 --> 00:04:57,330
I won't read out each of these,

74
00:04:57,330 --> 00:05:00,435
but shown here are
a few examples.

75
00:05:00,435 --> 00:05:02,565
There's different
ways that you can

76
00:05:02,565 --> 00:05:05,280
collapse some of these.

77
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:07,740
If there's leading zeros, well,

78
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,710
you can drop those leading zeros

79
00:05:10,710 --> 00:05:13,500
as shown in the highlighted
portion in the red,

80
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,720
and if there's several of

81
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:19,050
these hextets that
are all zeros,

82
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:24,570
you can replace those
consecutive zeros with colons,

83
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:27,990
and so if you see any
addresses in this form,

84
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:32,770
you should be able to
understand what they mean.

85
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:37,365
Now one of the important things

86
00:05:37,365 --> 00:05:41,595
is that structure
add scalability,

87
00:05:41,595 --> 00:05:43,140
and here we're referring to

88
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:45,240
not structure of the
messages necessarily,

89
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,345
but structure of the addressing.

90
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:51,945
In the Ethernet we
saw this concept of

91
00:05:51,945 --> 00:05:56,385
the vendors would get
the upper addresses.

92
00:05:56,385 --> 00:05:59,145
Upper bits of the address
would be assigned to a vendor,

93
00:05:59,145 --> 00:06:01,290
and then they would assign

94
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:03,660
to each device that
they manufactured,

95
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:06,930
the next, the lower bits
in those addresses.

96
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:11,295
Then they would ship
their devices worldwide.

97
00:06:11,295 --> 00:06:14,010
With this, you can
think of this as

98
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:16,590
a flat address that
it really doesn't

99
00:06:16,590 --> 00:06:22,680
have any meaning in terms of
how it's used in a network.

100
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:25,710
For that we saw in Ethernet,

101
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:30,300
where the Mac address
tables had one entry per

102
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:32,505
destination and
that's how it knew

103
00:06:32,505 --> 00:06:35,190
how to get to the destination,

104
00:06:35,190 --> 00:06:40,095
and that doesn't scale to
billions of devices worldwide.

105
00:06:40,095 --> 00:06:43,140
The solution is to

106
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:45,495
really add structure
to the addresses,

107
00:06:45,495 --> 00:06:48,490
so the devices can be grouped.

108
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:52,155
Grouping is called a subnet,

109
00:06:52,155 --> 00:06:56,595
which is a set of
consecutive IP addresses.

110
00:06:56,595 --> 00:07:00,555
The high-order bits in a
subnet are all the same.

111
00:07:00,555 --> 00:07:03,420
If we were to give,
we're showing an example

112
00:07:03,420 --> 00:07:06,600
here of these three laptops of

113
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:12,825
addresses 2.1.1.0,
2.1.1.2 and 2.1.1.3.

114
00:07:12,825 --> 00:07:18,015
They all share those
upper bits, the 2.1.1.

115
00:07:18,015 --> 00:07:21,165
They all share those
parts of the address.

116
00:07:21,165 --> 00:07:25,575
Then the low-order bits
can uniquely identify

117
00:07:25,575 --> 00:07:30,780
the particular node
within that network,

118
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:34,710
so the.1, the.2, the.3.

119
00:07:34,710 --> 00:07:38,340
Now, if somebody
needed to create

120
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:41,640
a table entry on

121
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:45,735
how to forward this traffic
to the destination,

122
00:07:45,735 --> 00:07:50,850
it might just need
those upper order bits,

123
00:07:50,850 --> 00:07:54,600
so anything in this
example that's going to

124
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:58,800
2.1.1 dot anything for

125
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,810
this particular
router, it can say,

126
00:08:00,810 --> 00:08:03,075
I'm just going to
send it to the right,

127
00:08:03,075 --> 00:08:08,955
out that particular port
and then a router closer to

128
00:08:08,955 --> 00:08:12,570
those end devices
would be able to

129
00:08:12,570 --> 00:08:17,500
know where within this
network should go.

130
00:08:17,870 --> 00:08:23,445
As mentioned, this node
is drew out all 32 bits,

131
00:08:23,445 --> 00:08:24,750
and so if you recall

132
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:29,400
the addresses IPv4
addresses, or 32 bits.

133
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:33,280
Here's the upper eight bits.

134
00:08:33,830 --> 00:08:39,780
We can represent the
decimal too as 00000010.

135
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:43,030
You separate it out like that.

136
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,330
Here these 24 bits or

137
00:08:48,330 --> 00:08:54,345
the up three decimal
places are all the same.

138
00:08:54,345 --> 00:08:57,060
Which then gets us to

139
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:01,020
the CIDR or cider is
how it's pronounced.

140
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:05,415
The classless
interdomain routing.

141
00:09:05,415 --> 00:09:11,625
Here you would see the
format of a.b.c.d/x.

142
00:09:11,625 --> 00:09:16,050
A Lot of times these
are called a prefix,

143
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:20,625
so x here represents the
length of the prefix,

144
00:09:20,625 --> 00:09:22,200
and so this is the number of

145
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,260
high-order bits that
are all the same,

146
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:27,030
and then the a, b,

147
00:09:27,030 --> 00:09:30,525
c and d are the values
which are the same.

148
00:09:30,525 --> 00:09:34,365
An example from the
previous slide,

149
00:09:34,365 --> 00:09:40,470
the 2.1.1.0/24 would
be that prefix.

150
00:09:40,470 --> 00:09:44,190
It says the 24 uppermost bits,

151
00:09:44,190 --> 00:09:46,935
which are represented by 2.1.1,

152
00:09:46,935 --> 00:09:48,980
are all going to be the same.

153
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:54,100
Then the zero, the lower order
end bits, we don't care.

154
00:09:54,100 --> 00:09:58,660
That's we're grouping
those all together.

155
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:02,260
We use a zero just
to represent that in

156
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:05,020
these listings that those

157
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:08,810
are all going to be
grouped together.

158
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:13,870
Another aspect of this

159
00:10:13,870 --> 00:10:17,260
is we have these
Cer addresses that

160
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:23,590
can group hosts and into
prefixes, and these subnets.

161
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:27,670
What's interesting is that
subnets can be combined.

162
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:31,135
Let's say on the left
we have two networks

163
00:10:31,135 --> 00:10:35,920
and they have two
different subnets,

164
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:45,070
1.00 and slash 24.11 1024.

165
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:50,180
This represents these 24
bits highlighted in red.

166
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:53,950
In each of the cases
are what all of

167
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:57,235
the addresses within each
of those subnets will be.

168
00:10:57,235 --> 00:11:00,535
Now if we had something

169
00:11:00,535 --> 00:11:03,640
a little closer to whoever
is going to be sending it,

170
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:05,245
on the right hand side, we have

171
00:11:05,245 --> 00:11:07,465
somebody that wants
to send something.

172
00:11:07,465 --> 00:11:09,340
What are the table
entries going to

173
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:10,960
need to look like there?

174
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:14,260
Well, do they need an entry
for each of the subnets?

175
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:17,545
Well, in any case,

176
00:11:17,545 --> 00:11:18,745
it's going to be, for

177
00:11:18,745 --> 00:11:22,135
either case it's going
to go to the left.

178
00:11:22,135 --> 00:11:24,370
If I send something to either of

179
00:11:24,370 --> 00:11:26,770
those subnets, it's
going to go left.

180
00:11:26,770 --> 00:11:29,905
What I can do is I can
actually group these.

181
00:11:29,905 --> 00:11:31,750
I can note that, well,

182
00:11:31,750 --> 00:11:34,825
it doesn't matter
which zero or one

183
00:11:34,825 --> 00:11:40,000
what that lower
order of the 24 bits

184
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,695
is and so I can actually
create a prefix and

185
00:11:44,695 --> 00:11:49,495
aggregate those
together to 1.100/23.

186
00:11:49,495 --> 00:11:54,160
Now it's only the 23
uppermost bits that

187
00:11:54,160 --> 00:12:00,010
are anything that matches
those that represents,

188
00:12:00,010 --> 00:12:05,150
basically, we're creating
this bigger grouping.

189
00:12:07,170 --> 00:12:11,710
This allows for hierarchy and is

190
00:12:11,710 --> 00:12:13,720
really one of the
key properties that

191
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:17,120
allows for scalability
worldwide.

192
00:12:17,610 --> 00:12:22,270
Now just diving into
the full header of

193
00:12:22,270 --> 00:12:26,590
IPV4 on the left and IPV6.

194
00:12:26,590 --> 00:12:31,540
You can see the source address,

195
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:35,440
you can see the destination
address of both of those,

196
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:38,095
and they're going to
be different sizes.

197
00:12:38,095 --> 00:12:41,815
We'll note something called TTL,

198
00:12:41,815 --> 00:12:47,620
which is the time to live
or in IPV6, hop limit.

199
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:51,160
If you recall the
problem of loops

200
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:55,015
and forwarding or
forwarding loops,

201
00:12:55,015 --> 00:12:58,310
then what happens is

202
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:03,445
we need to some mechanism
to be able to detect that.

203
00:13:03,445 --> 00:13:05,560
In Ethernet,

204
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:09,520
we saw something like
spanning tree protocol.

205
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:11,830
We didn't get into the details,

206
00:13:11,830 --> 00:13:13,960
but that was one example

207
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:16,300
of how we might be
able to handle it.

208
00:13:16,300 --> 00:13:19,435
In the Internet Protocol,

209
00:13:19,435 --> 00:13:22,840
the way it's handled is use
the time to live field.

210
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:25,585
Each hop is given packet,

211
00:13:25,585 --> 00:13:27,130
it decrements the time to

212
00:13:27,130 --> 00:13:29,980
live and if it ever
gets down to zero,

213
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:32,690
the packet just gets dropped.

214
00:13:33,630 --> 00:13:39,235
Then note the protocol
and next header field.

215
00:13:39,235 --> 00:13:42,250
The protocol field next title.

216
00:13:42,250 --> 00:13:44,185
Much as in Ethernet,

217
00:13:44,185 --> 00:13:47,140
we had that type
field that said what

218
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:50,695
was the thing that's
inside, what's the payload?

219
00:13:50,695 --> 00:13:53,005
How should I interpret
the payload?

220
00:13:53,005 --> 00:13:55,915
One of the types was IPV4.

221
00:13:55,915 --> 00:14:00,190
Well, here we have a
protocol field which says,

222
00:14:00,190 --> 00:14:04,520
now inside of the payload
of an IPV4 packet,

223
00:14:05,670 --> 00:14:09,085
how should I
interpret that data?

224
00:14:09,085 --> 00:14:11,905
Then it also has a check sum,

225
00:14:11,905 --> 00:14:16,790
so we could see a little
error handling in that.

226
00:14:17,010 --> 00:14:21,670
With that, that concludes
the introduction to

227
00:14:21,670 --> 00:14:26,870
the internet
protocol, and thanks.